[Music]  in this task your challenge will be to  build and design an aerodynamic glider  you'll have access to unlimited  resources but time is limited so choose  wisely and design carefully  [Music]  the mathematics of projectile motion  will be key in this challenge as you  design and build gliders be careful  though you'll need to balance the weight  and lift of your glider for the best  aerodynamics otherwise you won't win  this challenge so let's talk about  building a glider there's a bunch of  things that you'll get to choose in this  challenge and  a set of other things that really you'll  kind of be restricted on so i'm going to  kind of tell you those in hand right  firstly  when you build your glider and it might  look something like this uh the first  important thing is where is the center  of mass for your glider right in other  words if you were just carrying it the  center of mass or what we call the  barrier center you could actually let's  just see if i probably can't do it on  this you could balance your finger on it  let's find it and it would  stay exactly in place a bit like how a  basketball i might spin a ball and they  would have their finger in exactly the  right spot and it just spins around on  that to find the center of mass or  rather to  like where do you want the center of  mass of your glider you can actually  find the center of mass of a straight  object like say the fuselage of the  glider that you build by i'm doing  something really simple if you just take  two fingers  do this just move your fingers in  one of the cool things is what's slowing  down my fingers is friction  and the further away i am from the  center of mass the more friction is  applied so i'm not deliberately like  thinking about where i'm moving my  fingers i'm just moving them  independently of each other and they  eventually settle on the center of mass  right so physics kind of does that by  itself i didn't know like i didn't need  to measure that and even if there were  actually weights inside this so that it  wasn't evenly balanced i might measure  out and say oh i think it's there the  center of mass might be somewhere else  like i could have put masses here and  that process of just going to the middle  will find that center of mass  where do you want your center of mass to  be  you can put it anywhere but have a think  about what would happen if you had  you've got this made out of bolsa wood  for example you will have some other  things that you'll need to carry on your  glider some bits of metal and that kind  of thing which will weigh down your  aircraft you tell me what would happen  if the center of mass was near the front  of the glider  it's just going to nose dive isn't it  right so you want to distribute  your masses so that it's going to be  somewhere actually near the center more  specifically  have a think about where it is in  relation to the wings  probably just a little bit behind the  wings is the best place to go but you'll  actually be able to test that out okay  so that's the first thing center of mass  the second thing is  when you think about  how to get lift on your glider right  you've got your wings which you're going  to be making out of balsa wood but if  you just have them horizontally as air  transfers above and below you're not  going to get enough lift to actually fly  much of a distance so you want to have  this angled at a certain amount okay now  be as precise as you can i think what  you'll find is if this was the actual  fuselage which i've grossly exaggerated  then you want an angle of about five  degrees in here which isn't much so you  really need to measure carefully because  even if you're off by one degree right  in this case one degree is 20 so it can  really make a big difference yep  um you are going to make your fuselage  out of a piece of ball sword this part's  important you'll need to measure it  you're limited to 50 centimeters that's  the longest that you can make it but in  terms of the wingspan that's up to you  however i think you'll work out  eventually  that there's a natural limit to how wide  you can make this if you make it really  really wide think about this right  there's going to be more drag on the  front of this so it's going to slow you  down and you'll need to balance that out  yourselves and you can make those  decisions okay  now  i talked about choices that you can make  so this five degrees is one that i would  encourage you to get to as close as  possible but then you have some genuine  options depending on what your design  looks like and then how you want to fire  it so most of you probably know if we  think about projectile motion if you  have a flat surface and you're firing an  object off into the air number one  what's the shape of what's the name of  that shape  that's actually a parabola so you can  get that by a quadratic equation you  will always get a parabola of some kind  if you fire at a lower angle  you might go like this right and if you  fire a higher angle something like this  you're going to have a choice of what  angle of projection you fire at it's  going to be somewhere between 15 degrees  that's the lowest you can do and 45  which i don't think i've really drawn on  here that'll be the highest that you can  choose  you also can decide  how far or rather how much force you're  going to apply when you actually launch  your aircraft when you launch your  glider now you're not just going to be  throwing it okay that would be a bit  imprecise you're going to use an elastic  launcher so the more tension you put  into that elastic the harder it's going  to go maybe you don't want it to go  quite as hard because that's going to  make your um it's going to make your  aircraft fly up and then come back and  hit you you have to be quite careful  about that okay  when you are doing your design we're not  just going to look at how it flies we're  actually going to look at your  construction you want to think about  things like not just the wings which are  providing lift but these things back  here these sort of fins does anyone have  any ideas what purpose do they serve  fantastic so in order to have it  actually fly in a straight line things  like these actually provide stability to  your aircraft so we want to look for  things like that we want to also look  for things like symmetry this has been  designed and built and manufactured so  it's going to be symmetrical but you're  going to be creating your gliders by  hand so carefully designing measuring  out and then actually creating the  shapes of your materials you need to be  very cautious  i'm so excited to see what you build do  be careful because these these materials  do get damaged easily so you want to  factor that into your design as well  good luck teams let's start building 10.  this one here  seven  six five four  three  two  one

